"""Explain saved full-design diagnostics and matched-cohort sensitivity fits."""
import csv
import hashlib
import json
from pathlib import Path
import shutil
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
BASE=ROOT/'eval/outputs/releases/2026-09-29-graded-reporting'
DIAGNOSTICS=BASE/'diagnostics.json'
EFFECTS=BASE/'effects.csv'
OUT=ROOT/'ufscthesisx/ufscthesisx/chapters/collinearity_explanation.tex'
RELEASE=ROOT/'eval/outputs/releases/2026-10-11-collinearity-explanation'
diags={r['fit']:r for r in json.loads(DIAGNOSTICS.read_text()) if r['outcome']=='score'}
effects={(r['fit'],r['predictor']):r for r in csv.DictReader(EFFECTS.open())}
pooled=diags['pooled'];single=diags['pooled_single_spec_score']
assert pooled['n']==single['n']==diags['pooled_single_distance']['n']
for key,predictor in [('pooled_single_distance','distance'),('pooled_single_spec_score','spec_score')]:
 assert next(r for r in pooled['scaling'] if r['predictor']==predictor)==next(r for r in diags[key]['scaling'] if r['predictor']==predictor)
vd,vs=pooled['substantive_vif']['distance'],pooled['substantive_vif']['spec_score']
v_single=single['substantive_vif']['spec_score']
ifv=diags['components']['substantive_vif']['if_score']
consv=diags['components']['substantive_vif']['cons_score']
num=lambda fit,p:f"{float(effects[fit,p]['estimate']):.3f}"
text=r'''\paragraph*{Why check overlapping predictors?} The joint regression estimates distance and SPEC associations while also accounting for model and attack-language indicators. If those variables capture much of the same variation, the model has limited information with which to distinguish their separate contributions. For example, SPEC can differ systematically between checkpoints and languages; including indicators for those checkpoints and languages can therefore leave less distinct variation for its coefficient. Collinearity concerns this overlap among explanatory variables. Convergence instead concerns whether the fitting algorithm reached a solution: a converged fit can still have strongly overlapping predictors.

\paragraph*{What does VIF measure here?} For each substantive predictor, we temporarily regress its standardized values on the other columns of the fitted design, including the intercept and model/language indicators. The variance inflation factor is $\mathrm{VIF}=1/(1-R^2)$, where $R^2$ describes how well those other columns predict that predictor. A VIF of one indicates no linear overlap with the other columns; larger values indicate greater overlap. This is a diagnostic of the design matrix, not an additional model of StrongREJECT scores. In particular, the auxiliary $R^2$ does not describe the proportion of harmful-assistance scores explained. VIF also does not directly multiply the prompt-clustered GEE standard errors.

'''
text+=(f"In the pooled design, distance has VIF {vd:.2f}, whereas SPEC has VIF {vs:.2f} (Table~\\ref{{tab:graded-diagnostics}}). The latter corresponds to approximately {100*(1-1/vs):.1f}\\% of SPEC's observed variation being linearly predictable from the other design columns. This does not mean that SPEC is unrelated to the outcome; it means that its distinct adjusted contribution is harder to isolate. The overlap is with the full design, not necessarily just with distance. We use VIF greater than five as the trigger for single-predictor sensitivities, rather than as a universal boundary between valid and invalid models.\n\n")
text+=(f"\\paragraph*{{What does the condition number add?}} The condition number summarizes near-dependence across the entire design matrix, rather than for one predictor. Larger values indicate that some combinations of columns are harder to distinguish numerically. Its value depends on scaling and coding: here, continuous predictors use complete-cohort population-standard-deviation scaling, and the design retains the intercept and treatment-coded model/language indicators. The pooled value is {pooled['condition_number']:.1f}; it is {diags['components']['condition_number']:.1f} when IF and CONS replace SPEC. It complements VIF and is not interpreted using a separate acceptance cutoff.\n")
OUT.write_text('% Generated by eval/scripts/write_collinearity_explanation.py.\n'+text)
POST=OUT.with_name('collinearity_sensitivity_interpretation.tex')
text=(r'\paragraph*{What changes in the sensitivity fits?} The single-predictor fits remove SPEC from the distance model, or distance from the SPEC model, while retaining the model and language indicators. '
+f"The pooled comparisons use the same {pooled['n']:,} scored responses and the same predictor standardization, so their differences reflect the changed adjustment rather than a changed cohort. The distance coefficient changes from ${num('pooled','distance')}$ in the joint fit to ${num('pooled_single_distance','distance')}$ without SPEC; the SPEC coefficient changes from ${num('pooled','spec_score')}$ to ${num('pooled_single_spec_score','spec_score')}$ without distance (Table~\\ref{{tab:graded-single}}). Both retain their signs, but their magnitudes change. These are coefficients on the logit of expected score, not direct score increments.\n\n"
+f"SPEC's VIF remains {v_single:.2f} even when distance is omitted, showing that overlap with the retained model and language indicators is still substantial. In the decomposition, IF has VIF {ifv:.2f} and CONS has VIF {consv:.2f}; separating their coefficients also requires caution. Removing a predictor changes the question being estimated and can introduce omitted-variable bias. These checks therefore describe sensitivity; they do not recover a causal effect or guarantee an isolated predictor contribution.\n\n"
+r'\paragraph*{Implication for H\textsubscript{3}.} All reported fits pass convergence, finite-estimate and positive-definite covariance checks. Those establish numerical usability, not that the predictors provide independent explanations. Although standardization puts their coefficients on comparable relative scales, a larger absolute coefficient or smaller $p$-value alone cannot establish a stronger, more stable association. Interpretation must also consider predictor overlap, the changes under single-predictor adjustment, and the cohort sensitivities reported below.\n')
# The final raw-string paragraph uses a real trailing newline.
text=text.removesuffix('\\n')+'\n'
POST.write_text('% Generated by eval/scripts/write_collinearity_explanation.py.\n'+text)
RELEASE.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
for p in [OUT,POST,Path(__file__)]:shutil.copy2(p,RELEASE/p.name)
(RELEASE/'manifest.json').write_text(json.dumps({'purpose':'Explanation of existing graded diagnostics; no model refit.','sources':{str(p.relative_to(ROOT)):hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest() for p in [DIAGNOSTICS,EFFECTS]},'same_cohort_and_scaling_verified':True,'code_sha256':hashlib.sha256(Path(__file__).read_bytes()).hexdigest()},indent=2)+'\n')
